      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Ninety patients with old myocardial infarction were studied to evaluate the efficacy of intravenous dipyridamole 201Tl imaging for the detection of myocardial ischaemia in post-infarction patients, and to compare the prevalence of ischaemia in 63 patients with post-infarction angina (group I) and 27 patients without angina (group II). Thirty-four of the patients in group I and 15 of the patients in group II received coronary arteriography (CAG) for comparison; these were labelled groups IA and IIA, respectively. On 201Tl imaging, the incidence of scar with ischaemia in the infarct zone and scar with ischaemia at a distance were 72% and 42% in all patients, 89% and 52% in group I vs 33% and 19% in group II (P < 0.001 and P < 0.01, respectively). On CAG testing, the rates of infarct-related recanalization vessel and multi-vessel disease were 76% and 68% in group IA vs 40% and 40% in group IIA (P < 0.05 and P > 0.05, respectively). Thus, dipyridamole 201Tl imaging is a useful modality and post-infarction angina a proper indicator in the detection of myocardial ischaemia in post-infarction patients"},
